{
  "requirement": "Each requirement and example declaration badge has two adjacent links. Its\nkind (`req` or `example`) links to the declaration's anchor on the current\npage; its full name links to the evidence detail page. These segments stay\ntogether in one non-wrapping row, with no separate arrow link or plaintext evidence link.\nEach detail page links back to the declaration's anchored badge and to the\nindex. Reference badges continue to link to the declaration. These links\nresolve when a requirement name contains `.md`, as in\n`requirements.cli.mdbook`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The tests adequately cover each obligation in `requirements.index.navigation`.\n\n**Two adjacent links in the declaration badge**\n- `declaration_sections_contain_their_headers_and_markdown_bodies` checks the exact kind-link markup for both a req and an example. It uses `href=\"#habeas-{kind}-rules.{name}\"`, a title saying the link is within this chapter, and the aria-label.\n- The same test checks the name link to `requirements-index/rules.{name}.html` with the \"View evidence for\" title.\n- It also checks that both links sit in the summary header, not the body.\n- `mdbook_preprocessor_renders_definitions_between_stdin_and_stdout` checks that each `habeas-definition-badge` row has the `habeas-badge-kind` and `habeas-badge-name` classes and exactly two `</a>` elements. This covers the real binary path.\n- `declaration_badges_and_margin_controls_share_kind_colors` checks the name segment and that no `<svg` appears.\n\n**One non-wrapping row, with no arrow or plaintext evidence link**\n- `declaration_badge_stylesheet_keeps_split_links_in_one_row` asserts `inline-flex`, `flex-wrap: nowrap`, `white-space: nowrap` and `flex-shrink: 0` in the stylesheet.\n- The \"no separate arrow link\" clause is covered by asserting the header lacks `habeas-detail-icon`, and by the two-anchor count in the binary test.\n- The \"no plaintext evidence link\" clause is covered by asserting `See evidence` is absent in `definitions_without_a_marker_get_a_default_index_and_detail_pages` and `index_owns_details_in_book_order_with_badge_icons`.\n\n**Detail pages link to the anchored badge and to the index**\n- `definitions_without_a_marker_get_a_default_index_and_detail_pages` checks every detail page for `[Requirements index](../requirements-index.html)`. It also checks the `[View definition](…#habeas-req-…)` links for nested chapters.\n- `index_shows_evidence_states_and_links_generated_chapters` checks both links together in `[Requirements index](../requirements-index.html) · [View definition]`.\n- `index_owns_details_in_book_order_with_badge_icons` covers the example kind and a non-default index placement.\n\n**Reference badges continue to link to the declaration**\n- `references_in_nested_chapters_link_to_the_requirement_definition` checks impl, cite and test badges from a nested chapter. Each must link to `../requirements.html#habeas-req-requirements.ready`.\n\n**Links resolve when a name contains `.md`**\n- `links_preserve_requirement_names_containing_md` tests `chapter_href` directly. It covers the detail link and the definition link with an anchor, both with `%2Emd`.\n- `definitions_without_a_marker_get_a_default_index_and_detail_pages` and `index_owns_details_in_book_order_with_badge_icons` check the id, anchor href, detail href and \"View definition\" link for `cli.mdbook`.\n- The repository tests do not render a reference badge to a `.md`-named requirement. The reference badge builds its href through the same `chapter_href`, which `links_preserve_requirement_names_containing_md` tests with an anchor. I treat that as sufficient.\n\n**Escaping**\n- `definition_evidence_link_targets_are_escaped` covers escaping of the index path in the evidence link.\n\nI found no gap in any obligation.",
  "references": "4e0e60c4f8a54bb02a235630bf39f7a53d1b2376299a4958a791682aff9a7597",
